I = {
'electrical/amps-to-watts': "Watts measure the real power a load uses, while amps measure the current it draws. The two are linked by voltage — and, for AC loads such as motors, by power factor — so knowing any two lets you find the third when you size fuses, sockets, generators or inverters.",
'electrical/braking-resistor': "When a motor driven by a VFD slows down, it acts as a generator and pushes energy back into the drive's DC bus. A braking resistor burns off that energy as heat so the drive does not trip on over-voltage; see this guide to [VFD braking resistor sizing](ib:vfd-braking-resistor-sizing) and the common [causes of VFD overload trips](ib:vfd-overload-trip-causes).",
'electrical/cable-voltage-drop': "Every cable has resistance, so some voltage is lost between the supply and the load. Too much drop makes motors overheat and lights dim, which is why standards limit it to about 3–5%. Learn the method in this [voltage drop calculation](ib:voltage-drop-calculation) guide and how it fits with [choosing cable size for load current](ib:cable-size-for-load-current).",
'electrical/earth-conductor-size': "A protective earth conductor must carry the full fault current for the time it takes a breaker or fuse to trip — without overheating. The adiabatic equation gives the minimum safe size, as explained in this [earth conductor size calculation](ib:earth-conductor-size-calculation); the fault current itself comes from a [short circuit current calculation](ib:short-circuit-current-calculation).",
'electrical/earthing-resistance': "An earth electrode is only as good as its resistance to the ground around it. Lower resistance lets fault current flow safely and keeps touch voltages low. Soil resistivity and rod length matter most, as shown in this [earthing resistance calculation](ib:earthing-resistance-calculation) and the overview of [TN, TT and IT earthing systems](ib:tn-tt-it-earthing-systems).",
'electrical/induction-motor-slip': "An induction motor always turns a little slower than its rotating magnetic field — that difference is called slip, and it is what produces torque. Read how [an AC induction motor works](ib:how-ac-induction-motor-works) and see [induction motor slip explained](ib:induction-motor-slip-explained) in more detail.",
'electrical/kva-to-kw': "kVA is apparent power — what cables, transformers and generators must carry — while kW is the real power that does useful work. Power factor connects the two, as explained in this guide to [power factor, kW, kVA and kVAr](ib:power-factor-kw-kva-kvar) and [active, reactive and apparent power](ib:active-reactive-and-apparent-power).",
'electrical/kw-to-amps': "Knowing the current a load draws is the first step in choosing breakers, cables and contactors. The formula depends on whether the supply is DC, single-phase or three-phase — see [single-phase vs three-phase power](ib:single-phase-vs-three-phase-power) and [how to calculate three-phase power](ib:how-to-calculate-three-phase-power).",
'electrical/per-unit': "Power system engineers express impedances, voltages and currents as fractions of chosen base values. This per-unit method removes transformer ratios from the maths and makes fault studies much quicker, as shown in this introduction to the [per-unit system in power systems](ib:per-unit-system-power-system).",
'electrical/power-factor-correction': "Inductive loads such as motors draw reactive current that loads up cables and transformers without doing useful work. Adding capacitors supplies that reactive power locally, raising power factor and cutting your kVA demand. Learn [how to calculate capacitor bank kVAr](ib:how-to-calculate-capacitor-bank-kvar-formula) and the basics of [capacitor bank sizing for power factor correction](ib:capacitor-bank-sizing-power-factor-correction).",
'electrical/star-delta-transformation': "Some resistor and impedance networks cannot be simplified with series and parallel rules alone. The star-delta (Y-Δ) transformation swaps one three-terminal arrangement for an equivalent one, as explained in this [star-delta transformation formula](ib:star-delta-transformation-formula) guide.",
'electrical/thd': "Drives, UPSs, LED drivers and computers draw distorted current that adds harmonics to the supply. Total harmonic distortion (THD) sums them into one figure you can compare with IEEE 519 limits. Read [power harmonics explained](ib:power-harmonics-explained) and how to do a [THD calculation for power systems](ib:harmonic-distortion-thd-calculation-power-systems).",
'electrical/three-phase-motor-power': "A motor's nameplate kW is its shaft output, but the supply must also cover losses and reactive current. That is why the current drawn is higher than kW alone suggests. See how to work out [motor full-load current (FLC)](ib:motor-full-load-current-flc) and [how to calculate three-phase power](ib:how-to-calculate-three-phase-power).",
'electrical/transformer-current': "A transformer's kVA rating and voltage set the full-load current on each winding — the figure used to size breakers, fuses, cables and busbars. Learn [how to calculate transformer kVA rating](ib:how-to-calculate-transformer-kva-rating) and why switching on causes [transformer inrush current](ib:transformer-inrush-current).",
'electrical/transformer-efficiency': "Transformers lose a little energy as core (iron) loss whenever they are energised and as copper loss that grows with load. Efficiency depends on the balance between the two, as covered in this [transformer efficiency calculation](ib:transformer-efficiency-calculation) guide.",
'electrical/transformer-short-circuit': "The impedance of a transformer limits how much current can flow when a fault occurs on its secondary. Knowing the maximum fault current is essential for choosing breakers with enough breaking capacity — see this guide to [short circuit current calculation](ib:short-circuit-current-calculation).",
'electrical/transformer-turns-ratio': "A transformer changes voltage in proportion to the number of turns on its windings, and changes current the other way. This guide to the [transformer turns ratio and secondary voltage](ib:transformer-turns-ratio-secondary-voltage) explains the relationship, and turns ratio is one of the standard [transformer routine tests](ib:transformer-routine-and-type-tests).",
'electrical/ups-sizing': "A UPS must be big enough in both kVA and kW for everything connected to it, with room to grow. Learn more about [UPS capacity (kVA) calculation](ib:ups-capacity-kva-calculation) and which [UPS topology](ib:ups-topology-comparison) suits your load.",
'electrical/voltage-unbalance': "When the three phase voltages are not equal, motors draw unbalanced currents and run hotter — even a small voltage unbalance causes a much larger current unbalance. This guide to [voltage unbalance calculation for motors](ib:voltage-unbalance-calculation-motors) explains the NEMA method and its effect on motor rating.",
'electronics/555-timer': "The 555 is one of the most popular chips ever made — a simple timer that can blink LEDs, generate square waves and create time delays with just a few resistors and a capacitor. See the [555 timer IC explained](ib:555-timer-ic-explained) for how it works inside.",
'electronics/adc-resolution': "An analog-to-digital converter turns a voltage or current into a number, and its bit depth sets the smallest change it can see. Read [analog-to-digital conversion explained](ib:analog-to-digital-conversion-explained) and how this affects [analog signal resolution in PLCs](ib:analog-signal-resolution-in-plc).",
'electronics/boost-converter': "A boost converter steps a DC voltage up — for example from a 5 V USB supply to 12 V — by storing energy in an inductor and releasing it at a higher voltage. See the [boost converter working principle](ib:boost-converter-working-principle) and the related [SEPIC converter](ib:sepic-converter-working-design).",
'electronics/buck-converter': "A buck converter steps a DC voltage down efficiently by switching it on and off and smoothing the result with an inductor and capacitor — far less wasteful than a linear regulator. Learn the [buck converter working principle](ib:buck-converter-working-principle) and why [capacitor ESR](ib:capacitor-esr-equivalent-series-resistance) matters for output ripple.",
'electronics/capacitor-energy': "A capacitor stores energy in the electric field between its plates, and the amount rises with the square of the voltage. Read about the [capacitor working principle](ib:capacitor-working-principle) and how [supercapacitors](ib:supercapacitor-working-principle) store far more.",
'electronics/capacitor-lifetime': "Aluminium electrolytic capacitors wear out as their electrolyte slowly dries, and heat speeds this up dramatically. They are often the first part to fail in power supplies and drives — see this [electrolytic capacitor lifetime calculation](ib:electrolytic-capacitor-lifetime-calculation) and how the [failure modes of electrolytic, ceramic and tantalum capacitors](ib:electrolytic-vs-ceramic-vs-tantalum-capacitor-failure-modes) differ.",
'electronics/current-divider': "When current reaches parallel branches, it splits between them — more through the lower resistance. The [current divider rule](ib:current-divider-rule-explained) follows directly from [Kirchhoff's current law](ib:kirchhoffs-current-law-kcl-explained) and is the basis of [shunt resistors in instrumentation](ib:shunt-resistors-in-instrumentation).",
'electronics/heat-sink': "Power transistors, MOSFETs and regulators turn some energy into heat, and too much heat shortens their life or destroys them. A heat sink with low enough thermal resistance keeps the junction cool — see this [heat sink calculation](ib:heat-sink-calculation) guide.",
'electronics/inductor-energy': "An inductor stores energy in its magnetic field while current flows, and releases it when the current changes — which is why switching off a coil can cause a voltage spike. Learn the [inductor working principle](ib:inductor-working-principle) and the limits set by [inductor saturation current](ib:inductor-saturation-current).",
'electronics/led-resistor': "LEDs need their current limited, or they burn out within seconds. A series resistor is the simplest way to do it — read about the [LED resistor calculation](ib:led-resistor-calculation) and the [LED working principle](ib:led-working-principle).",
'electronics/op-amp-gain': "Operational amplifiers are the building blocks of analog electronics, and two resistors set their gain. Start with [op-amp basics](ib:operational-amplifier-op-amp-basics), the [voltage follower](ib:voltage-follower-unity-gain-buffer-explained), and how an [instrumentation amplifier differs from an op-amp](ib:instrumentation-amplifier-vs-op-amp-key-differences).",
'electronics/pcb-trace-width': "A copper track on a circuit board heats up as current flows through it, so it must be wide enough for the current it carries. The IPC-2221 formula links current, temperature rise and track size; for vias, see this guide to [PCB via current capacity](ib:pcb-via-current-capacity).",
'electronics/pwm-duty-cycle': "Pulse width modulation controls power by switching a supply fully on and off very fast, varying the on-time to set the average. It drives motors, dims LEDs and is at the heart of [VFDs](ib:vfd-working-principle-explained) — see [pulse width modulation and duty cycle](ib:pulse-width-modulation-duty-cycle).",
'electronics/rc-filter': "A resistor and capacitor make the simplest filter in electronics — removing noise from sensor signals or blocking DC from audio. Read about [RC low-pass and high-pass filters](ib:rc-low-pass-high-pass-filter) and how [Sallen-Key active filters](ib:active-filter-sallen-and-key) give a sharper response.",
'electronics/reactance': "In AC circuits, inductors and capacitors oppose current in a way that depends on frequency — this is reactance. Combined with resistance it gives impedance and a phase shift between voltage and current, as explained in [impedance and reactance in AC circuits](ib:impedance-reactance-ac-circuits) and [phase angle in AC circuits](ib:phase-angle-in-ac-circuits).",
'electronics/resistor-color-code': "Most through-hole resistors are too small to print a value on, so coloured bands show their resistance and tolerance instead. This [resistor color code guide](ib:resistor-color-code-guide) explains how to read them.",
'electronics/resonant-frequency': "An inductor and a capacitor together resonate at one frequency, where their reactances cancel. This is how radios tune to a station and how many oscillators and filters work — see the [LC resonant circuit frequency](ib:lc-resonant-circuit-frequency) explained.",
'electronics/snr': "Signal-to-noise ratio compares the signal you want with the unwanted noise riding on it, usually in decibels. It sets how small a change you can reliably measure; see this [signal-to-noise ratio guide](ib:signal-to-noise-ratio-snr-calculator), [decibels in electronics](ib:decibel-in-electronics-db-dbm) and [what signal integrity means](ib:what-is-signal-integrity).",
'electronics/voltage-divider': "Two resistors in series split a voltage in proportion to their values — the voltage divider. It is used everywhere to scale signals and set reference levels, as covered in the [voltage divider rule explained](ib:voltage-divider-rule-explained), which follows from [Kirchhoff's voltage law](ib:kirchhoffs-voltage-law-kvl-explained).",
'electronics/wheatstone-bridge': "A Wheatstone bridge measures very small resistance changes by balancing two voltage dividers against each other. It sits inside strain gauges, load cells, pressure sensors and RTD circuits — see these [Wheatstone bridge applications](ib:wheatstone-bridge-10-applications).",
'engineering/4-20ma-scaling': "The 4–20 mA current loop is the standard analog signal in process instrumentation: 4 mA means 0% of the range and 20 mA means 100%. Converting between current and process value is daily work for instrument engineers — see the [4 to 20 mA current loop explained](ib:4-to-20-ma-current-loop-explained) and [PLC analog scaling from raw counts](ib:plc-analog-scaling-raw-count).",
'engineering/control-valve-kv-cv': "The flow coefficient (Kv or Cv) tells you how much liquid a valve passes at a given pressure drop, and it is the starting point for sizing a control valve. Read about the [control valve flow coefficient](ib:control-valve-flow-coefficient) and the [basic requirements for control valve sizing](ib:control-valve-sizing-basic-requirements).",
'engineering/darcy-weisbach-head-loss': "Friction between a moving fluid and the pipe wall causes a pressure drop that pumps must overcome. The Darcy–Weisbach equation is the most accurate general method — see [how to calculate pressure drop in pipes](ib:calculate-pressure-drop-in-pipes) and its link to [Bernoulli's equation](ib:bernoullis-equation-formula).",
'engineering/dp-flow-square-root': "Differential pressure flow meters such as orifice plates produce a DP proportional to flow squared, so the signal needs square-root extraction to read flow. Learn about [square root extraction in DP flow measurement](ib:square-root-extraction-dp-flow) and the [common mistakes in DP flow measurement](ib:mistakes-in-dp-flow-measurement).",
'engineering/flow-meter-k-factor': "Pulse-output flow meters such as turbines, vortex meters and gear meters send a fixed number of pulses per unit volume — the K-factor. It converts pulse frequency into flow rate and pulse count into totals, as explained in these [flow meter K-factor calculations](ib:flow-meter-k-factor-calculations).",
'engineering/gas-flow-correction': "Gas volume changes with pressure and temperature, so the same mass of gas can be very different volumes. Flows are corrected to normal or standard conditions to compare them — see [actual flow vs standard flow vs normal flow](ib:actual-flow-standard-flow-normal-flow).",
'engineering/heat-duty': "Heat duty is the rate at which heat is added to or removed from a fluid — the key figure for sizing heat exchangers, heaters and coolers. It depends on mass flow, specific heat and temperature change.",
'engineering/horizontal-tank-volume': "In a horizontal cylindrical tank, the liquid volume does not rise in step with level, because the tank is widest in the middle. Converting a level reading into volume needs the circular-segment formula — see [tank volume from level measurement](ib:tank-volume-from-level-measurement).",
'engineering/hydraulic-cylinder-force': "Hydraulic cylinders turn fluid pressure into large linear forces for presses, excavators and machines. The force depends on pressure and piston area, and is lower when retracting because the rod takes up part of the area.",
'engineering/hydrostatic-pressure': "The pressure at the bottom of a liquid column depends only on its height and density — the principle behind most tank level transmitters. See [hydrostatic level measurement](ib:hydrostatic-level-measurement), [DP transmitter level measurement](ib:dp-transmitter-level-measurement) and when to use [zero elevation and zero suppression](ib:zero-elevation-and-zero-suppression).",
'engineering/loop-resistance': "A loop-powered transmitter needs a minimum voltage at its terminals, and every resistor, barrier and cable in the loop uses some of the supply. Checking the [instrument loop impedance](ib:instrument-loop-impedance-4-20ma-accuracy) makes sure the [4–20 mA loop](ib:4-to-20-ma-current-loop-explained) still works at full scale and at [NAMUR NE 43](ib:namur-ne43-standard-explained) fault currents.",
'engineering/mass-flow-rate': "Mass flow is what matters for reactions, billing and energy balances, because it does not change with temperature or pressure the way volume does. Read the [difference between mass flow and volumetric flow](ib:difference-between-mass-flow-and-volumetric-flow) and how a [thermal mass flow meter works](ib:thermal-mass-flow-meter-working).",
'engineering/orifice-flow': "The orifice plate is the most widely used flow element in industry: a thin plate with a hole that creates a pressure drop proportional to flow squared. Learn about [orifice plate flow measurement](ib:orifice-plate-flow-measurement), the [orifice plate sizing guide](ib:orifice-plate-sizing-guide) and how it compares in [flow nozzle vs orifice plate vs Venturi](ib:flow-nozzle-vs-orifice-plate-vs-venturi).",
'engineering/pipe-flow-velocity': "Fluid velocity in a pipe decides pressure drop, noise, erosion and the risk of water hammer. Designers keep velocity within recommended ranges by choosing the right pipe size for the flow.",
'engineering/pitot-tube-velocity': "A Pitot tube measures flow velocity from the difference between total and static pressure. It is used for air ducts, stacks and aircraft, and an [averaging Pitot tube](ib:averaging-pitot-tube-working-principle) extends the idea to pipe flow measurement.",
'engineering/pt100-resistance': "A Pt100 is a platinum resistance thermometer with 100 Ω at 0 °C; its resistance rises predictably with temperature. Learn the [Pt100 vs Pt1000 differences](ib:rtd-pt100-vs-pt1000-differences), the [Callendar–Van Dusen equation](ib:callendar-van-dusen-equation-rtd) behind this calculator, and [how to test an RTD](ib:how-to-test-an-rtd-pt100).",
'engineering/pulley-speed': "Belt and pulley drives change the speed and torque between a motor and a machine. The pulley diameters set the speed ratio, and the shaft centre distance sets the belt length.",
'engineering/pump-power': "The power a pump needs depends on how much liquid it moves, how high it lifts it and how efficient it is. This sets the motor size and the energy cost — and the type of pump matters too, as compared in [centrifugal vs positive displacement pumps](ib:centrifugal-vs-positive-displacement-pump).",
'engineering/reynolds-number': "The Reynolds number tells you whether flow in a pipe is smooth (laminar) or chaotic (turbulent), which affects pressure drop, heat transfer and flow meter accuracy. See [what Reynolds number is](ib:what-is-reynolds-number) and why it matters in flow measurement.",
'engineering/thermal-expansion': "Most materials grow when heated and shrink when cooled. Pipes, rails, bridges and machine parts must allow for this movement, or the stresses can bend, crack or break them.",
'engineering/torque-power-speed': "Torque, speed and power are linked for any rotating shaft: power equals torque times angular speed. This lets you find motor torque from its kW and rpm, or the power needed to turn a load at a given speed.",
'engineering/venturi-flow': "A Venturi tube narrows a pipe smoothly to a throat, where the fluid speeds up and its pressure drops; the pressure difference gives the flow rate. See the [Venturi tube flow meter](ib:venturi-tube-flow-meter) explained and how it compares in [flow nozzle vs orifice plate vs Venturi](ib:flow-nozzle-vs-orifice-plate-vs-venturi).",
'engineering/weir-flow': "A weir is a notch or barrier in an open channel; the height of water flowing over it gives the flow rate. Weirs are common for effluent and irrigation, and are often paired with level transmitters — see [measuring flow rate with level sensors](ib:measure-flow-rate-level-sensors).",
'physics/acceleration': "Acceleration is how quickly velocity changes — speeding up, slowing down or changing direction. It links speed and time in every motion problem, from a car pulling away to a ball falling under gravity.",
'physics/buoyancy': "Any object in a fluid is pushed upward by a force equal to the weight of fluid it displaces — Archimedes' principle. It explains why ships float, why balloons rise and why things feel lighter underwater.",
'physics/centripetal-force': "Anything moving in a circle needs a force pulling it towards the centre — centripetal force. It keeps cars on curves, satellites in orbit and water in a spinning bucket.",
'physics/coulombs-law': "Electric charges push or pull on each other with a force that grows with the charges and falls with the square of the distance between them. Coulomb's law describes this force, the foundation of electrostatics.",
'physics/decibel': "Decibels express ratios of power, voltage or sound on a logarithmic scale, so huge ranges fit into manageable numbers and gains simply add. See [decibels in electronics: dB and dBm](ib:decibel-in-electronics-db-dbm) for practical examples.",
'physics/density': "Density is mass per unit volume — how tightly matter is packed. It identifies materials, decides whether things float and is a key input for flow and level measurement; see [what specific gravity is](ib:what-is-specific-gravity) and how [Coriolis meters measure density](ib:coriolis-density-measurement).",
'physics/doppler-effect': "When a sound source moves towards you, its pitch rises; when it moves away, the pitch falls. The Doppler effect is used in radar, medical ultrasound and [Doppler ultrasonic flow meters](ib:transit-time-vs-doppler-ultrasonic-flow-meter).",
'physics/escape-velocity': "Escape velocity is the speed an object needs to break free of a planet's gravity without any further push. It depends only on the planet's mass and radius — about 11.2 km/s for Earth.",
'physics/force': "Newton's second law says force equals mass times acceleration. It is the core equation of mechanics, connecting what pushes or pulls an object with how its motion changes.",
'physics/free-fall': "In free fall, gravity is the only force acting, so every object speeds up at the same rate — about 9.81 m/s² near Earth's surface. This lets you work out fall time, distance and impact speed.",
'physics/gravitational-force': "Every mass in the universe attracts every other mass. Newton's law of universal gravitation gives the strength of that pull, from an apple falling to the Moon orbiting Earth.",
'physics/heat-conduction': "Heat flows through solids from hot to cold at a rate set by the material's thermal conductivity, thickness and area. Fourier's law is the basis of insulation design and heat exchanger calculations — see [what thermal conductivity is](ib:what-is-thermal-conductivity) and the main [types of heat exchangers](ib:types-of-heat-exchangers).",
'physics/hookes-law': "The force needed to stretch or compress a spring is proportional to how far you move it — Hooke's law. It describes springs, scales and the elastic behaviour of materials.",
'physics/ideal-gas-law': "The ideal gas law, PV = nRT, links the pressure, volume, temperature and amount of a gas. It works well for most gases at everyday conditions and underlies gas flow and tank calculations.",
'physics/kinetic-energy': "Kinetic energy is the energy of motion. Because it grows with the square of speed, doubling speed quadruples the energy — which is why stopping distances rise so steeply.",
'physics/momentum': "Momentum is mass times velocity — a measure of how hard it is to stop a moving object. It is conserved in collisions, making it a powerful tool for analysing impacts.",
'physics/ohms-law': "Ohm's law, V = I × R, is the most important relationship in electrical work: voltage, current and resistance are tied together. Start with the [basics of Ohm's law](ib:basics-of-ohms-law) and the [electrical units of measure](ib:electrical-units-of-measure-explained).",
'physics/orbital-period': "How long a satellite or planet takes to go round depends only on the mass it orbits and the size of its orbit. Kepler's third law gives the period of the ISS, GPS satellites, geostationary orbits and the planets.",
'physics/pendulum': "A simple pendulum swings with a period that depends on its length and gravity, not on how heavy the bob is. This regularity made pendulums the heart of accurate clocks for centuries.",
'physics/potential-energy': "Gravitational potential energy is the energy an object has because of its height. Lifting a mass stores energy that is released as it falls — the principle behind hydropower and pumped storage.",
'physics/pressure': "Pressure is force spread over an area. It explains why sharp knives cut and why wide tyres don't sink, and it is one of the most measured quantities in industry — see [absolute vs gauge pressure](ib:absolute-vs-gauge-pressure), [Pascal's law in pressure instruments](ib:understanding-pascals-law-pressure-instruments) and the [U-tube manometer](ib:u-tube-manometer-working-formula).",
'physics/projectile-motion': "A thrown ball, a cricket shot or a water jet all follow a curved path called a parabola. Projectile motion splits the movement into steady horizontal travel and vertical motion under gravity.",
'physics/rc-time-constant': "When a capacitor charges or discharges through a resistor, the voltage changes exponentially with a time constant τ = RC. It sets timing in circuits, filters and delays — see [RC and RL time constants explained](ib:rc-and-rl-time-constant-explained) and their use in [RC snubber design](ib:snubber-circuit-rc-design).",
'physics/resistors-series-parallel': "Resistors in series add up, while resistors in parallel give a total lower than the smallest one. Combining them is the first step in analysing any circuit — see [series vs parallel circuits explained](ib:series-vs-parallel-circuits-explained).",
'physics/snells-law': "Light bends when it passes from one material into another because its speed changes. Snell's law predicts the angle of bending — and the critical angle beyond which light is totally reflected, as in optical fibres.",
'physics/speed-distance-time': "Speed is distance divided by time. This simple relationship answers everyday questions — how long a journey takes, how fast you need to go or how far you will travel.",
'physics/suvat-equations': "The SUVAT equations describe motion with constant acceleration using five quantities: displacement, initial velocity, final velocity, acceleration and time. Knowing any three lets you find the other two.",
'physics/thin-lens': "Lenses and curved mirrors form images at distances set by their focal length. The thin lens equation predicts where the image appears and how large it is — the basis of cameras, glasses, microscopes and projectors.",
'physics/torque': "Torque is the turning effect of a force — force multiplied by the distance from the pivot. It explains why long spanners loosen tight bolts and how levers multiply force.",
'physics/wavelength-frequency': "For any wave, speed equals wavelength times frequency. This links the colour of light, the pitch of sound and the channel of a radio signal to their wavelengths.",
'physics/weight': "Weight is the force of gravity on a mass, so it changes from planet to planet while mass stays the same. A 70 kg person weighs about 687 N on Earth but only 114 N on the Moon.",
'physics/work-and-power': "Work is done when a force moves something, and power is how fast that work is done. These ideas connect forces and energy to the watts of motors, engines and people.",
}
